# Packaging the Mermaid trope

`@athrio/loom-trope-mermaid` carries the Mermaid trope of Part XIV to other books. A book installs it with `loom add mermaid`, and the registry reads the trope's looms from the store, as the chapter "Tropes a workspace installs" describes. The package holds those looms and nothing else: a trope that only declares kinds has no code to build or load.

The looms have one source, the chapters of Part XIV in this book. npm packs only files inside the package's folder, and a tangle writes the code of a section, never a loom whole. So the package's `prepack` script copies Part XIV's looms into a `looms` folder in the package just before npm packs it, and Git ignores that copy.

## The manifest [package.json]

The `files` field ships only the `looms` folder, and `publishConfig` makes the scoped package public. The `repository` field points npm at this package's folder in the monorepo, which the provenance signature checks against the workflow that publishes it. The package shares Loom's version, as every package Loom releases does, so the store installs the trope that matches the Loom installing it. The `prepack` script replaces the `looms` folder with a fresh copy of Part XIV's looms, so a publish never ships a stale one.

{{ version = ${version} }}

=>

{
  "name": "@athrio/loom-trope-mermaid",
  "version": "::[version]",
  "description": "The Mermaid trope for Loom: lets a book draw a diagram in a box of the Mermaid kind.",
  "license": "MIT",
  "repository": {
    "type": "git",
    "url": "git+https://github.com/athrio-com/loom.git",
    "directory": "packages/loom-trope-mermaid"
  },
  "files": [
    "looms"
  ],
  "publishConfig": {
    "access": "public"
  },
  "scripts": {
    "prepack": "rm -rf looms && mkdir looms && cp ../../corpus/14-mermaid-diagrams/*.loom looms/"
  }
}

## What Git leaves out [.gitignore]

The `looms` folder is a copy that `prepack` makes, so Git ignores it.

=>

looms/
